# A float has no `hash`: a NaN is unequal to itself and +0.0 equals -0.0, so no
# hash of a float agrees with its `==`, and a Map or a Set keyed on one would
# lose entries or keep duplicates. Each float key below is refused where the
# container is named, and so is asking a float for its hash.
main: function is {
    s: (SetVal f64)
    print "\{s.length}"
    m: (MapVV f32 v: u64)
    print "\{m.length}"
    r: (MapVR f64 V: String)
    print "\{r.length}"
    x: 1.5
    print "\{x.hash}"
}
